--- body ---
## Context
Every IETF RFC carries a mandatory Security Considerations section. The
specification chain has none, and the omission is not a formality here.

In 11 862 words the only privacy content is REQUIREMENTS.md's line
recording that four privacy requirements were DROPPED when hosted mode went
out of scope (D2), plus DESIGN.md's note that the returned hosted-mode
lines — contract keying, multi-tenant consent bookkeeping,
encryption-at-rest, quota arithmetic — "stay dead". That reasoning is sound
for the threats it names and it does not cover the local case. Dropping
hosted mode removes multi-tenant exposure. It does not remove the exposure
of a system that indexes the whole of a user's personal library on their own
machine.

The concrete surfaces the design already has: a derived index over every
item, note and annotation the user holds; passage text that may leave the
machine for an embedding API; a query endpoint and a status endpoint; a
local database on a disk the design explicitly treats as the user's (C3);
credentials for whatever remote services are configured.

This ticket does not assert that any of these is mishandled. It asserts
that the chain is silent, and that silence is indistinguishable from
"considered and found safe" to any reader — including the author in six
months and any reviewer upstream.

## Placement — ratified 2026-08-29
Standalone `SECURITY.md`, not a section of DESIGN.md or CONSTRAINTS.md. The
ruling and its grounds are in DECISIONS.md: threats outlive designs, a section
inside DESIGN.md is rewritten with the design it sits in, and a threat model
states obligations we take on where CONSTRAINTS.md states facts the world
imposes. FIELD-REVIEW.md supplies the supporting evidence — of 39 surveyed
projects none documents a threat model, and the survey records three live
privacy failures of exactly this class.

## Actions
1. Enumerate the assets: what the system holds that a user would mind
   losing or leaking. Be concrete about the derived index — it is a
   reconstruction of the library, not merely a pointer to it.
2. Enumerate the surfaces: local database and its file permissions, the
   query and status endpoints and what they bind to, any egress to
   embedding or LLM providers, credential storage, logs.
3. For each, state the design's current answer — including "none yet", where
   that is the truth. An honest gap is the point of the document.
4. Cross-reference R- and C-numbers rather than restating them.

## Test
No unit test fits a prose section. The check is a review-gate item: the
document names every egress point the design has, verified by reading
DESIGN.md section 2 for network calls and enumerating them independently.
A positive control is required — seed a known egress and confirm the sweep
finds it — otherwise "no egress found" and "I did not look" read alike.

## Verification
- [x] Placement ratified in DECISIONS.md before the text is written
- [ ] Every egress point in DESIGN.md section 2 appears in the enumeration
- [ ] Gaps stated as gaps, not smoothed over
- [ ] No requirement or threshold restated; cross-references only

## Invariants
- This document describes and discloses. It does not decide: a new
  obligation is a requirement and goes through DECISIONS.md then
  REQUIREMENTS.md.
- The D2 hosted-mode ruling is not reopened. Local-only is the scope.

## Exit criteria
- [x] Placement decided and ratified (standalone SECURITY.md)
- [ ] Assets, surfaces and current answers enumerated, gaps included
- [ ] Registered in CLAUDE.md and README.md
- [ ] `make check` passes
